[Music]  50 years ago six women Runners made a  decision that would change the sport  Forever at the first New York City  marathon where women were allowed to  compete instead of running when the race  officially began they sat protesting  inequitable treatment of men and women  in the race five decades later some of  the women are still running but humble  about their impact on the sport  last month in Central Park employees of  the New York Road Runners Club gathered  at the former starting line for the New  York City marathon  to recreate and celebrate an iconic  photograph  [Applause]  a snapshot of six women that changed the  sport of running forever we sat down  with Jane murky Pat Barrett Lynn  Blackstone and Nina Cusick four of the  women from the group that became known  as the six who sat we wanted to make an  impression and we wanted to get things  changed  [Applause]  1972 was the first year women were  allowed to run in the New York City  marathon with just a handful enter but  the amateur athletic Union or AAU which  governed amateur sports required an  altered course or start time for the  women so they were scheduled to begin 10  minutes ahead of the men I mean how  crazy did that seem to you well that's  what didn't make any sense yeah I mean I  was kind of young I didn't understand it  all for six people that didn't make any  sense you know so the six women at the  racist start took a stand by taking a  seat and the New York Times took notice  how was it decided that you were going  to do this protest and sit at the start  of the race I mean it didn't seem fair  to Nina and Pat they were running to  have to adjust their times and the men  that were running were very supportive  and it was really just to show the AAU  that it could be done and it made sense  earlier that year the AAU had lifted its  ban preventing women from competing in  distance races altogether Catherine  Switzer had helped break the gender  barrier at the Boston Marathon I was  amazed in talking to Catherine Switzer  when she told me that a doctor had said  her uterus was going to fall out if she  ran  did any of you  I heard that you heard that one too I've  heard coaches say that yeah  despite how popular running is now it  wasn't always that way women Runners  were subject to harassment in the old  days sometimes people throw things at  you I mean really oh constantly making  or make a terrible I mean it was at the  time of women's Liberation but men were  and young boys they were constantly  making fun of women running if you ran  on the street or or a wow look at those  legs like cat calls yeah and men were  criticized too the marathon was just  starting to  if you weren't coming to what what it is  today I mean it was you know a lot of  men um they even got uh had got hassled  trying to run marathons  [Applause]  after  26.2 miles Nina Cusick won the 1972 New  York City marathon for the women its  first Women's Champion she spoke with  CBS News after how did you feel coming  around that last line  I really felt very good until the last  half of my my legs got a little tight  what do you think about what keeps you  going in that last half mile when you  feel like that  well all kinds of things keep you going  you always wonder who might be coming up  behind you you also have a time in mind  that you're you know trying to finish in  and there's always that negative fear  that you're maybe forced to quit shortly  after the race the AAU dropped it's  separate but equal starting requirement  the six who sat helping change the  course of running insisting on equity in  opportunity  [Applause]  this year nearly 24  000 women are registered to run in the  New York City marathon I hope  development is absolutely amazing never  think of it you know because I or we had  something to do it just the the  development the Evolution for example we  were just talking uh a while ago that  about scholarships and things that are  now available to women yeah it's just  wonderful it's empowering it's it has  helped Empower a lot of it right  you know it's so interesting some of  those women help fight to get the  marathon in the Olympics it has changed  so drastically I thought it was great  too Nina and Pat who finished that race  one and two they talked about the fact  that they had 10 minutes added on to  their time because remember they were  supposed to start right 10 minutes  earlier but they were sitting for those  first 10 minutes in protest but you know  it's moments like this that we have to  remember this is where we came from and  50 years ago wasn't that long ago so  kudos to them and kudos to the New York  Road Runners you know for being there  for these women all along and good luck  to all those men and women

